<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Financial Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as International Financial Control</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Financial Commission was a body of representatives of the great powers set up in 1898 to supervise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s public finances. Imposed after Greece's state bankruptcy of 1893 and its defeat in the Greco-Turkish War of 1897, it collected certain state revenues, such as monopolies and customs, to pay the war indemnity to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and service foreign loans. It remained a lasting symbol of foreign control over the Greek state's finances.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Foreign oversight of Greek finances had a precedent: between 1857 and 1859 the UK, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> set up an International Financial Commission of Inquiry to report on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s ability to repay the debt of 1833, though the intervention of Russia kept it to an advisory role. Creditors had also imposed a financial commission on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> after its bankruptcy of 1869, and in 1881 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-public-debt-administration">Ottoman Public Debt Administration</a>, with 5,000 employees, after its default of 1875.</p><p>In 1893 the government of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charilaos-trikoupis">Charilaos Trikoupis</a> declared bankruptcy; more than half of the state's revenue that year went on servicing loans. Years of fruitless talks followed. Defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1897">Greco-Turkish War</a> of 1897, in which Turkish forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>, then forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, under the Treaty of Constantinople of 4 December 1897, to pay the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> 4,000,000 Ottoman liras, and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandros-zaimis">Alexandros Zaimis</a> had to seek new loans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Negotiations with representatives of the UK, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> began in October 1897 and led to Law 2519 of 23 February 1898, which established the six-member commission. The great powers guaranteed a loan of 151,300,000 French francs, from which 93,900,000 went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and the rest to the existing state debt, the deficit of 1897 and the costs of issue. To repay it, the commission took over the revenue of the monopolies on salt, oil, matches, playing cards, cigarette papers and Naxos emery, the tobacco tax, the stamp duties and duties from the Port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>, and assessed state agencies for their efficiency in collecting taxes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The commission remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> until 1936 and played a key part in administering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> lending of the 1920s under the League's Economic and Financial Organization. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the expression International Financial Control came to stand for foreign supervision of the country's public finances in general. Some observers have judged the experience not wholly negative for public administration, since it raised state revenue and reduced the outflow and misappropriation of capital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “International Financial Commission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
